Sogen: closing a device's last handle cancels the I/O pending on it

When a file's last handle closes, Windows sends its device
IRP_MJ_CLEANUP, and the device completes every request still pending
on the file; for a socket afd.sys completes them cancelled. Sogen did
that for named pipes alone. A socket closed under a pending receive
dropped the request: its event was never signaled and the thread
waiting on it waited for good.

warm.lisp closes its WebSocket client's connection while the client's
read thread is in a ReadFile on the socket; the read thread never woke,
and the dump that follows refused to run with it alive ("Cannot save
core with multiple threads running").

Every device's pending requests now end with its file's last handle.
Only named pipes and AFD endpoints keep requests pending.

diff --git a/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/object.cpp b/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/object.cpp
index 3c34a52..e88325c 100644
--- a/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/object.cpp
+++ b/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/object.cpp
@@ -92,7 +92,9 @@ namespace sogen
                 auto* device = c.proc.devices.get(h);
                 if (device && device->ref_count == 1)
                 {
-                    if (device->is_last_file_reference() && device->get_internal_device<named_pipe>())
+                    // The file's last handle ends the I/O still pending on it, as IRP_MJ_CLEANUP does: its
+                    // waiters see the requests cancelled instead of waiting on what nothing will complete.
+                    if (device->is_last_file_reference())
                     {
                         device->cancel_io(c.win_emu, 0);
                     }
